<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · artifact</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kuttamuwa stele</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 735 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century-bce">8th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Kuttamuwa stele is a basalt funerary monument found in 2008 at Zincirli in south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, ancient Sam'al. Set up around 735 BCE by Kuttamuwa, an official of King Panamuwa II, it shows him at a funerary feast, and its inscription asks that offerings be made for his soul, which dwells in the stone. It is important evidence for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> beliefs about the soul.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kuttamuwa-stele.jpg" alt="Kuttamuwa stele" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dosseman · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gaziantep_Archaeology_museum_Kuttamuwa_stele_4270.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>The stele was found at Samʾal in 2008 by the Neubauer Expedition of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oriental-institute">Institute for the Study of Ancient Cultures</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-chicago">University of Chicago</a>. It is a basalt slab weighing about 360 kg, some 0.91 m tall and 0.61 m wide, and it carries an inscription in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aramaic">Aramaic</a>. Kuttamuwa, a royal official of the 8th century BCE, ordered it while he was still alive, to be set up upon his death.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The text opens in his own voice: he is Kuttamuwa, servant of Panamuwa, who commissioned the stele for himself during his lifetime, placed it in an eternal chamber and established a feast there. The offerings named are a bull for Hadad Qarpatalli, rams for Šamš, for Hadad of the Vineyards and for Kubaba, a ram for a further deity whose name is uncertain, and a ram for his soul, written NBŠ, which is to be in the stele.</p><p>The inscription then turns to the future owners of the chamber, whether his own sons or anyone else's. Each year they are to take from the best produce of the vineyard as an offering, to perform the slaughter close to his soul, and to set aside a cut of the leg for him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The stele asks mourners to commemorate Kuttamuwa's life and afterlife with feasts for the soul that dwells in the stone. It is one of the earliest references in a Near Eastern culture to the soul as an entity separate from the body, and for that reason it holds a particular place in the study of ancient beliefs about death and remembrance.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kuttamuwa stele” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Kuttamuwa stele</b>, found in 2008, shows a belief in a soul dwelling in the stone.</blockquote><a href="/state/samal">Sam'al</a></li></ul></section>
